Held content — data stores and reference libraries.

<section #l1-data | `<data>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `storage-hosts` · eHTML `<data>` · JATS `<no direct equivalent>` · `content` `structured`

*No kwargs or boolean flags.*

Produced by: canonical `<data>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code-block #code:l1-data-1>
<data>
  <library format=bibtex>
    @article{goodall2024,
      author = {Goodall, Jane},
      title = {The Effect of Elephants on Climate},
      journal = {Nature},
      year = {2024}
    }
  </library>
</data>
</code-block>

*Structural / front-matter — shown as source. It carries metadata or apparatus (configuration, stored data, document front matter), not standalone body content, so there is no in-flow live box.*

*A library block in BibTeX format. The library plugin parses this, registers entries in the citation system. The &lt;data&gt; block itself produces no rendered output.*

A `<data>` block can also store a **dataset** — a payload (CSV/TSV/JSON/…) held under an id with a `<dataset>` — that a consuming element pulls in by `@id`. A `<dataset>` is always authored in the **long form** `<dataset …>…</dataset>`; the pipe form would truncate the payload at the first `>`, which tabular/diagram/code data routinely contains. Here a CSV dataset is stored once and a `<table src="@id" />` renders it as a grid:

<code-block #code:l1-data-2>
<data>
  <dataset #parts-stock format=csv>
part,code,in_stock
hex bolt,#HB-08,120
flat washer,#FW-04,340
lock nut,#LN-06,210
</dataset>
</data>

<table src="@parts-stock" />
</code-block>

<data>
  <dataset #parts-stock format=csv>
part,code,in_stock
hex bolt,#HB-08,120
flat washer,#FW-04,340
lock nut,#LN-06,210
</dataset>
</data>

<table src="@parts-stock" />

*Unlike the front-matter examples above, this one renders a live result: the table grid above is produced from the stored dataset. The dataset is held verbatim as opaque data — never interpreted as markup, so the `#`-prefixed part codes appear as literal cell text — and the consuming element (here, a `&lt;table&gt;`) decides how to present it. (The `&lt;data&gt;`/`&lt;dataset&gt;` declaration itself renders nothing; only the table does.)*

The `<table>` is not the only reader. The same opaque store feeds several consumers, each interpreting the one payload its own way: `<table src="@id" />` renders a grid, `<diagram engine src="@id" />` reads the bytes as its engine source (a Mermaid or abc diagram), `<code src="@id" />` renders them as a verbatim inline code listing, and `<code-block src="@id" />` renders them through `<pre><code>` — the whitespace-preserving form, so **multi-line code keeps its indentation** (an inline `<code>` is reflowed, so a multi-line one is warned toward a code block). An `@id` that is unresolved, or the wrong kind for the reader (a diagram whose dataset format hint disagrees with its engine, say), renders a visible asset-error rather than failing silently. Storage is opaque; interpretation belongs to the consuming element.

</section>

<section #l1-library | `<library>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `storage-hosts` · eHTML `<library>` · JATS `<no direct equivalent (entries lift into ref-list)>` · `content` `opaque` → `<parsed entries (registered in citation system)>`

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `format` | kwarg | `bibtex` `csl-json` `ris` `endnote-xml` `other` | Attribute-form equivalent of the format-word positional. When both the positional and the kwarg are omitted, the library-load plugin auto-detects the format via citation-js (works reliably for BibTeX and CSL-JSON). A named format is passed to citation-js as a forceType. |
| `src` | kwarg |  | External source (#133): a filesystem path, an http(s) URL, or any reachable URL (e.g. a GitHub raw link). The reference data is loaded from there and parsed exactly as inline content (same formats; never injected as markup). With src= the node's own (empty) body is ignored; inline and src libraries are both valid and multiple of either merge. A failed load (unreachable / 404 / CORS-blocked / parse-fail) renders a visible error and never aborts the document (always-renders). Filesystem paths apply on the CLI/build; in the browser a relative path resolves against the document base URL and is fetched (cross-origin URLs are CORS-limited). A URL source needs an async render (the CLI render command / the browser renderAsync); a synchronous render flags it. |

Produced by: canonical `<library>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code-block #code:l1-library-1>
<library format=bibtex>
  @article{goodall2024,
    author = {Goodall, Jane},
    title = {The Effect of Elephants on Climate},
    journal = {Nature},
    year = {2024}
  }

  @book{darwin1859,
    author = {Darwin, Charles},
    title = {On the Origin of Species},
    publisher = {John Murray},
    year = {1859}
  }
</library>
</code-block>

*Structural / front-matter — shown as source. It carries metadata or apparatus (configuration, stored data, document front matter), not standalone body content, so there is no in-flow live box.*

*A BibTeX library block. The parser reads the entries and registers goodall2024 and darwin1859 in the citation registry. Citations elsewhere (e.g., &lt;cite goodall2024&gt;) resolve against these entries. The library block itself produces no rendered output.*

<code-block #code:l1-library-2>
<library format=csl-json>
  [
    {
      "id": "goodall2024",
      "type": "article-journal",
      "author": [{"family": "Goodall", "given": "Jane"}],
      "title": "The Effect of Elephants on Climate",
      "container-title": "Nature",
      "issued": {"date-parts": [[2024]]}
    }
  ]
</library>
</code-block>

*Structural / front-matter — shown as source. It carries metadata or apparatus (configuration, stored data, document front matter), not standalone body content, so there is no in-flow live box.*

</section>
